    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d327">

                <pb facs="0437" n="369" xml:id="pg369"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">462.11 T 41/13½</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">President Wilson to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-04-05">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">5 April, 1915</hi>.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: I had already seen this
                    memorandum of Mr. Lansing’s.<anchor n="16" xml:id="d327-f1"/> I appreciate its
                    force to the full. But it ought not to alter our course so long as we think
                    ourselves on the firm ground of right.</p>
                <p>It has, as Mr. Lansing points out, its bearings on the Thrasher case. That case,
                    as I said the other day, troubles me. I should very much like to know what Mr.
                    L’s view of it is. We must compound policy with legal right in wise proportions,
                    no doubt.</p>
                <p>Faithfully Yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>W. W.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
